speechtext:
There seems to be a misapprehension on this subject. If any hon. member will turn to section 14 of the Naturalization Act, he will find the authorities before whom the oaths of allegiance and naturalization shall be taken and who will grant the certificates of residence or service and whatever further is necessary. They are as follows :
A judge of a court of record in Canada;
A commissioner authorized to administer oaths in any court of record in Canada;
A commissioner authorized by the Governor General to take oaths under this Act;
A justice of the peace of the county or district where the alien resides;
A notary public;
A stipendiary magistrate, or police magistrate.

This is not a provision with regard to the place where the

)

application is to be made. It is a special provision in regard to the naturalization of aliens who have been in Canada for a long time. Under section 52 of the Act aliens who were in Canada before confederation, or in Rupert's Land before July 15, 1870, or in British Columbia before July 20, 1871, or In Prince Edward Island before July 1, 1873, that is, before these provinces respectively came into confederation, can now become naturalized by simply taking oaths before justices of the peace and filing those oaths in certain offices. The question is as to what offices these oaths may be filed in, and be of record.

 Ques. of Order (Mr. Johnston) 2383;
Ruling (Mr. Speaker) 2383 (ii).
Commdtteas, Organization of: Remarks (Mr. R. L. Borden) 507, 560 (d).
